== (i) the 1+1 horizon cut: S = (1/6) ln L + const ==
    L      S(L)        S - (1/6) ln(chord)   [chord = (2n/pi) sin(pi L/n)]
      64   0.780091        -0.028510
     128   0.894754        -0.029161
     256   1.009108        -0.029488
     512   1.121075        -0.029654
    1024   1.222661        -0.029737
  fitted coefficient of ln(record depth) = 0.16622   target c/6 = 0.16667   err = 4.4e-04
  -> S - (1/6) ln(depth) is CONSTANT (drift ~1e-3 over 16x in L):
     the ledger capacity sealed behind a single horizon seam grows
     by exactly ONE SIXTH OF A NAT PER E-FOLD of record depth.

== (ii) 2d record lattice: wedge entropy is extensive in cut area ==
   n = 12: cut length 12, S = 0.71263
   n = 16: cut length 16, S = 0.99701
   n = 20: cut length 20, S = 1.28525
   n = 24: cut length 24, S = 1.57573
   n = 28: cut length 28, S = 1.86758
  linear fit S = 0.07222 * n + -0.15667; max residual = 2.7e-03
  -> the sealed capacity is EXTENSIVE IN THE CUT AREA (the record
     area law): entropy per unit horizon area is a lattice constant
     (nonuniversal), the LAW is the linearity.

== (iii) capacity quanta: the modular ledger is discrete ==
    L     eps_1     eps_2     eps_3     spacing   2pi^2/ln(cL)
    128   1.31706   4.01510   6.85455    2.84249
    512   1.11440   3.37710   5.71901    2.34528
  one-constant log law: c fixed at L = 128 gives c = 8.104;
  predicted spacing at L = 512: 2.36947   measured: 2.34528   err = 2.4e-02
  -> the modular ledger behind the seam is DISCRETE (capacity
     quanta), with spacing closing only logarithmically in record
     depth: Delta eps = 2 pi^2 / ln(c L) - the boost log-box of
     p13b.  This is P7's C5 discreteness direction, realized at
     finite record scope.
